    "Body state machine: `AccelBody` must preserve HTTP body semantics: trailers pass through, `size_hint` remains conservative after transforms, non-data frames are not copied, errors become `tonic::Status`, and in-flight offload futures are polled without blocking executor threads.",
    "Buffer policy: DSA `copy_crc` needs stable source/destination memory while hardware runs. The adapter hides pooling/pinning/coalescing decisions and should avoid offload for tiny frames where setup cost dominates.",
    "Service compatibility: the layer must preserve `NamedService` when manually applied to generated services, or be installed through `Server::builder().layer(...)` where Tonic already wraps services.",
    "Protocol placement: this sees gRPC bytes after codec/framing has produced HTTP body frames, not protobuf messages. It can accelerate byte transforms such as copy/CRC/compression, but it cannot make message-aware schema decisions."
  ],
  "tradeoffs": [
    "Pros: clean cutover outside Tonic codec internals; matches existing `accel-middleware` purpose; composes with Tower; can work for any Tonic service using `http_body::Body<Data = Bytes>`; keeps Tokio as the scheduler/backpressure boundary.",
    "Pros: easier A/B testing than codec forks: add/remove one layer, tune thresholds, or use software fallback without regenerating proto services.",
    "Cons: less semantic visibility than codec-level integration. It sees byte frames, not prost encode/decode objects, so opportunities tied to protobuf layout or per-message allocation remain outside this design.",
    "Cons: frame coalescing can add latency and memory pressure. A strict threshold/max-buffer policy is mandatory or large streaming RPCs can be harmed.",
    "Cons: DSA completion polling/wake behavior is the hard part. If implemented as wake-on-every-poll like current minimal `idxd_async`, it can burn CPU and erase accelerator gains; Tokio-facing submitter must own that policy."
  ]
}

